From ed6da986e18a702ffa54fe57d8d06026fb366300 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <florian.stosse@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:29:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] typing getters: look up the normalized term

An application of a logic function whose body reads memory, such as
`len(s)` with `logic integer len(char *s) = s[0] == '\0' ? 0 : 1;`, is
replaced by `Logic_normalizer` with an application of a copy of the function
specialized for the `Here` label. `Typing.type_term` types the normalized
term and memoizes the result under it, but the getters `get_number_ty`,
`get_cast` and `get_effective_ty` (and so `get_typ` and `get_effective_typ`)
looked up the term they were given. Callers that pass the original term
aborted the translation with
"typing was not performed on construct len(s)":

- `Loops.handle_annotations`, when such an application is the whole term
  of a loop variant: `loop variant len(s);`;
- `Translate_utils.env_of_li`, when such an application is bound by a
  `\let`, in any annotation: `assert \let k = len(s); k >= 0;`.

Normalize the term in the getters, as `Interval.get_from_profile` already
does. A term that the normalizer does not replace is looked up as before.

The loop variants are now translated. The `assert \let` no longer aborts:
the memory-tracking analysis does not support that application and the
annotation is ignored with the existing "not yet supported" warning, as
for other unsupported constructs.

The new test `constructs/logic_app_reading_memory.c` covers both loop
variant cases.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
